\DOC BYTELIST_DIGITIZE_TAC

\TYPE {BYTELIST_DIGITIZE_TAC : string -> term -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Replaces an assumption stating that a bytelist read of a memory region equals a
term by an explicit list of freshly named byte variables plus one per-byte
memory-read assumption.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument {s : string} is the prefix used to name the generated byte
variables, which are numbered from 0 upwards with this prefix. The second
argument {tm : term} is the bytelist read expression, of the form {read (memory
:> bytelist (base,n)) state}, whose assumption in the goal is to be digitized.
The tactic finds the assumption {tm = u}, discards it, and in its place puts an
assumption equating the explicit cons-list of the new variables {x1 ... xn} to
{u}, where n is the byte count of the region, together with n further
assumptions, one per byte, each stating that the single-byte read at the base
address offset by the corresponding literal word equals the matching variable.
All other assumptions and the conclusion are unchanged.
{
   A, tm = u ?- t
   ================================================  BYTELIST_DIGITIZE_TAC s tm
   A, [x1; ...; xn] = u,
      (byte-read assumptions for x1 ... xn) ?- t
}

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, always on
goals whose relevant assumption is a bytelist read of a concrete-size region in
a fixed state.

\ENDDOC
